This chapter specifies what `morloc make` produces and how the result runs. A
compiled program is a manifest describing its commands, one pool per language
holding the generated code, and a launcher. At run time the nexus reads the
manifest, starts the pools, and routes calls and data between them. The
chapter defines the process lifecycle, the protocol the processes speak, the
shared memory and binary formats that data travels in, the worker and
streaming models, and the data flow through the CLI's collection, parsing and
rendering stages.

